<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carlota of Mexico</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1840">1840</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Charlotte of Belgium, Empress Carlota</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carlota of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, born <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princess-charlotte-of-wales">Princess Charlotte</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-of-belgium">Leopold I of Belgium</a> and the wife of Maximilian, with whom she reigned as empress of Mexico from 1864 to 1867. In 1866 she travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to seek help for the failing empire from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a>, but was refused and suffered a mental breakdown. She spent the rest of her long life in seclusion in Belgium.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carlota-of-mexico.jpg" alt="Carlota of Mexico" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J. Malovich, Trieste · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carlota_de_mejico.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charlotte was born on 7 June 1840 at the Palace of Laeken, the fourth child and only daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-of-belgium">Leopold I of Belgium</a> and Louise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>. Her first name honoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princess-charlotte-of-wales">Princess Charlotte of Wales</a>, her father's first wife. Leopold, at first disappointed by a daughter, came to treat her as his favourite child. Through her mother she was a granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis Philippe I</a>, and through her father a first cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, and she spent long holidays in the French royal residences and at Windsor Castle.</p><p>In 1856 two suitors sought her hand, Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-of-saxony">George of Saxony</a> and King Pedro V of Portugal, the favourite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> and Leopold. She declined Pedro, writing that crowns were heavy burdens. She married Maximilian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, whom his brother Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a> appointed Viceroy of Lombardy-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a> in September 1857. In Milan Maximilian soon neglected her, and she complained of loneliness and boredom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> selected Maximilian as figurehead of a proposed French empire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and Charlotte overcame her husband's doubts. A Mexican delegation, persuaded by conservatives who wrongly promised broad support, offered the crown, and the couple were informally proclaimed at Miramare Castle on 10 April 1864. After a blessing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 19 April, they reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>. Maximilian let her rule as regent during his absences, so she is considered the first woman to rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> ordered the withdrawal of French military aid, the imperial couple's position became untenable. On her own initiative Charlotte sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, landing at Saint-Nazaire on 8 August 1866. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-cloud">Saint-Cloud</a> she met the ailing Napoleon III on 11 August, and later the Pope, and both refused her. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> her mental health seemed to fail, and a doctor advised confining her at Miramare Castle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Maximilian was deposed and executed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a> in June 1867, and Charlotte, not knowing she was a widow, was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. She learned of his death in January 1868. She lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tervuren">Tervuren</a>, Laeken and, from 1879, Bouchout Castle in Meise, for 48 years in a poor mental state. She saw only family, embroidered, played cards and listened to a gramophone. The cause of her illness is uncertain; some authors suspect poisoning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p><p>Charlotte died at Bouchout on 19 January 1927, aged 86, of pneumonia after influenza. Six former Belgian legionaries who had served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> carried her coffin on 22 January, and she was buried in the Royal Crypt of the Church of Our Lady of Laeken. Much of her fortune was managed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> and was used to finance the Congo colonial enterprise.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charlotte of Belgium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Juan Nepomuceno Almonte headed a regency until Maximilian and his wife <b>Carlota</b> of Belgium arrived in May 1864.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-mexican-empire">Second Mexican Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Laeken, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. Died in Meise, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.87,4.35&amp;year=1896" aria-label="Laeken on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Carlota of Mexico"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5235 5770h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5235 5770h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.87,4.35&amp;year=1896">What was here in 1896? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
